> What does leftjoin give for unmatched rows? missing?
**assistant**:
> Yes, when you perform a `leftjoin` in Julia using the DataFrames package, any unmatched rows from the left DataFrame (in this case, `df1`) will result in `missing` values for the columns from the right DataFrame (in this case, `df2_grouped`). 
> 
> Here's a brief explanation of how it works:
> 
> - **Matched Rows**: For rows in the left DataFrame (`df1`) that have corresponding matches in the right DataFrame (`df2_grouped`), the joined row will contain values from both DataFrames.
> 
> - **Unmatched Rows**: For rows in `df1` that do not find a match in `df2_grouped`, the columns from `df2_grouped` in the resulting DataFrame will contain `missing` values.
> 
> This behavior is similar to how `left_join` works in R's `dplyr`. It effectively allows you to retain all the information from the left DataFrame while adding data from the right, leaving gaps (`missing`) where no corresponding data exists. 
> 
> If you want to handle these `missing` values in any specific way (e.g., filling with zeros or other values), you can use the `coalesce` function or similar methods. Let me know if you need examples on how to do that!
