> I have added a line
> sumweights2.skill[ismissing.(sumweights2.skill)] .= 0.01
> But sumweights2.skill still shows up as type Vector{Union{Missing, Float64}}. How can I make it just Vector{Float64}?
**assistant**:
> The issue you're encountering is because the array still has missing values, which make the type `Vector{Union{Missing, Float64}}`. To convert it to `Vector{Float64}`, you'll need to replace the `missing` values with the specified value (in your case, `0.01`) and then convert it to a `Vector{Float64}`.
> 
> You can achieve this by using the `coalesce` function, which replaces `missing` values with a specified value and can help ensure that the resulting array has a uniform type. Here's how you can do it:
> 
> ```julia
> using DataFrames
> 
> # Example: replacing missing values and converting to Float64
> sumweights2.skill = coalesce.(sumweights2.skill, 0.01)  # Replace missing with 0.01
> sumweights2.skill = Float64.(sumweights2.skill)          # Convert to Vector{Float64}
> ```
> 
> After executing this, `sumweights2.skill` should be of type `Vector{Float64}` without any `missing` values.
> 
> ### Explanation:
> 1. **`coalesce.(sumweights2.skill, 0.01)`**: This replaces each `missing` value in `sumweights2.skill` with `0.01`.
> 2. **`Float64.(...)`**: This ensures that the resulting array is strictly of type `Float64`.
> 
> If you want to directly modify the values in-place without creating a new variable, you can do this:
> 
> ```julia
> sumweights2.skill .= coalesce.(sumweights2.skill, 0.01)  # Directly in-place
> ```
> 
> Using `.=`, it updates the existing array while preserving its reference, and should help maintain the attribute you need. Let me know if you need further assistance!
